Here’s a clear, interview-ready explanation of wait() vs sleep() in Java 👇

1️⃣ sleep() – Pause a Thread
What it does

Pauses the currently executing thread for a fixed time

Does NOT release any lock (monitor)

After time expires, thread becomes Runnable

Where it belongs

Thread.sleep()

Static method of Thread class

Example
synchronized (this) {
    Thread.sleep(2000);
    // lock is still held here
}

2️⃣ wait() – Inter-Thread Communication
What it does

Makes the thread wait until notified

Releases the lock on the object

Used with notify() / notifyAll()

Where it belongs

wait() is a method of Object class

Must be called inside a synchronized block

Example
synchronized (this) {
    wait(); // releases lock
    // resumes after notify()
}

3️⃣ Key Differences (Very Important)
| Feature            | `wait()`                   | `sleep()`       |
| ------------------ | -------------------------- | --------------- |
| Class              | `Object`                   | `Thread`        |
| Releases lock      | ✅ Yes                      | ❌ No            |
| Needs synchronized | ✅ Yes                      | ❌ No            |
| Wakes up by        | `notify()` / `notifyAll()` | Time expiry     |
| Purpose            | Thread coordination        | Pause execution |

4️⃣ What Happens to Lock? (Core Concept)
sleep()
synchronized(obj) {
    Thread.sleep(1000);
}


Lock is NOT released

Other threads BLOCKED

wait()
synchronized(obj) {
    obj.wait();
}


Lock is released

Other threads can enter synchronized block

5️⃣ Illegal Usage (Interview Trap ⚠️)
❌ This will throw exception:
wait();   // IllegalMonitorStateException

✅ Correct:
synchronized(this) {
    wait();
}

6️⃣ Real-World Example
Producer–Consumer (Simple)
synchronized (queue) {
    while(queue.isEmpty()) {
        queue.wait();
    }
    consume();
}


Producer:

synchronized (queue) {
    produce();
    queue.notify();
}

7️⃣ One-Line Interview Answer

wait() releases the lock and waits for notification, while sleep() pauses the thread for a fixed time without releasing any lock.

🟢 Summary

Use sleep() for timing/delays

Use wait() for thread communication

Never mix them blindly